      "units": [
        {"unit": "U41", "title": "When I Was Young", "canDo": "I can tell story about past", "dialoguePlace": "Gitega Home", "readingText": "When Mary was five years old, she lived in Gitega with her grandmother. While her grandmother was cooking beans, Mary was playing outside. She was running with her brother. Suddenly, it started to rain. Mary was laughing. Her grandmother was calling: Come inside! They sat near the fire. Her grandmother told a story about a clever rabbit. Mary listened carefully. She still remembers that story. She says: When I was young, my grandmother taught me many stories. Those stories helped me love Burundi and its traditions. Now Mary is 26, a teacher, and she tells those stories to her students.", "readingWords": 138, "vocab": ["While", "Suddenly", "Remember", "Tradition", "Story"], "grammar": "While I was playing", "tryAtHome": "Tell 1 story from when you were young, record"},
        {"unit": "U42", "title": "The Day I Learned to Swim", "canDo": "I can tell learning story", "dialoguePlace": "Lake Tanganyika", "readingText": "When I was eight, my father taught me to swim in Lake Tanganyika. At first, I was afraid. The water was deep. My father held my hand. He said: Breathe slowly. Kick your legs. I tried. I drank water. I coughed. Everyone laughed. But I did not give up. After one week, I could swim alone. I felt proud. Learning needs courage. Now as an adult, I teach my students not to give up.", "readingWords": 78, "vocab": ["Afraid", "Deep", "Breathe", "Courage", "Proud"], "grammar": "At first / After one week", "tryAtHome": "Tell about something you learned"},
        {"unit": "U43", "title": "My First Day at Work", "canDo": "I can talk about first day", "dialoguePlace": "School", "readingText": "I still remember my first day at work as a teacher. I was nervous. My mother held my hand and encouraged me. I saw many students. My head teacher smiled. She said: Welcome, Mary. I sat with my colleague Sara. We became friends. That day, I taught my first lesson. I was so happy. First days are scary but beautiful. John remembers his first day in his shop too.", "readingWords": 70, "vocab": ["Nervous", "Welcome", "First day", "Smiled", "Scary"], "grammar": "I was nervous / She smiled", "tryAtHome": "Tell about first day at work"},
        {"unit": "U44", "title": "A Difficult Exam", "canDo": "I can talk about challenges", "dialoguePlace": "Classroom", "readingText": "Last term, I had a difficult English exam for my teaching certificate. I studied every evening. But on exam day, I forgot everything. My heart was beating fast. I took a deep breath. I read the questions slowly. I remembered. I answered. I passed. I learned that calm helps. When you are stressed, breathe. John also passed his business exam.", "readingWords": 66, "vocab": ["Difficult", "Exam", "Forgot", "Calm", "Passed"], "grammar": "I had / I forgot", "tryAtHome": "Tell how you handle stress"},
        {"unit": "U45", "title": "Helping a Stranger", "canDo": "I can tell moral story", "dialoguePlace": "Market", "readingText": "One day at the market, I saw an old man. He was carrying a heavy bag. He was tired. I helped him. I carried his bag to the bus. He thanked me. He said: God bless you. I felt happy all day. Helping a stranger is a small act with big joy. John also helps customers in his shop.", "readingWords": 62, "vocab": ["Stranger", "Heavy", "Tired", "Bless", "Joy"], "grammar": "He was carrying", "tryAtHome": "Help someone and tell story"},
        {"unit": "U46", "title": "My Role Model", "canDo": "I can describe role model", "dialoguePlace": "Home", "readingText": "My role model is my mother. She wakes up early. She works hard. She sells vegetables at the market. She paid for my school. She is honest. She never gives up. She teaches me to be kind and strong. I want to be like her. As an adult teacher, I follow her example.", "readingWords": 60, "vocab": ["Role model", "Honest", "Hard work", "Strong", "Kind"], "grammar": "She wakes up / She works", "tryAtHome": "Describe your role model"},
        {"unit": "U47", "title": "A Dream I Had", "canDo": "I can talk about dreams", "dialoguePlace": "Bedroom", "readingText": "Last night, I had a strange dream. I was flying over Burundi. I saw hills, lakes, and rivers. I saw my school from above. I landed in Gitega. Everyone was clapping. I woke up smiling. Dreams can inspire us. My dream is to help Burundi grow. John dreams to grow his business too.", "readingWords": 60, "vocab": ["Dream", "Flying", "Strange", "Inspire", "Above"], "grammar": "I was flying", "tryAtHome": "Tell a dream you had"},
        {"unit": "U48", "title": "Learning from Mistakes", "canDo": "I can talk about mistakes", "dialoguePlace": "Work", "readingText": "Once I made a mistake in my lesson plan. I was afraid to tell my head teacher. I hid it. Later I felt guilty. I told the truth. She forgave me. I learned that honesty is better than fear. Mistakes are teachers. We grow when we admit them. John also learns from his business mistakes.", "readingWords": 60, "vocab": ["Mistake", "Guilty", "Honest", "Forgave", "Admit"], "grammar": "I made a mistake / I told the truth", "tryAtHome": "Tell about a mistake you fixed"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "B1.2",
      "theme": "Work & Money",
      "units": [
        {"unit": "U49", "title": "Small Business", "canDo": "I can talk about business", "dialoguePlace": "Market Stall", "readingText": "My aunt has a small business. She sells samosas near the school. Every morning, she buys flour, oil, and potatoes. She makes 100 samosas. She sells each for 500 FBu. She saves some money. She pays school fees for her children. Small business needs discipline. If you save, you grow. John learns from her.", "readingWords": 62, "vocab": ["Business", "Profit", "Save", "Discipline", "Fees"], "grammar": "She sells / She saves", "tryAtHome": "Calculate profit of small sale"},
        {"unit": "U50", "title": "My Work - John's Shop", "canDo": "I can talk about work", "dialoguePlace": "Shop", "readingText": "John works in his shop in Bujumbura. He arrives at 8am. He arranges products. He helps customers. He learns to count money quickly. At the end of the month, he earns 500,000 FBu. He buys books. He saves the rest. Work teaches responsibility. Mary visits his shop to practice English with customers.", "readingWords": 62, "vocab": ["Job", "Earned", "Customers", "Responsibility", "Arranged"], "grammar": "He worked / He earned", "tryAtHome": "Describe a job you want"},
        {"unit": "U51", "title": "Saving Money", "canDo": "I can talk about saving", "dialoguePlace": "Home", "readingText": "Saving money is important. If you get 100,000 FBu, save 20,000 FBu. Do not spend all. Keep it in a safe place. Small savings become big. My grandmother says: A little each day fills the basket. I save for my school project. John saves for his business.", "readingWords": 56, "vocab": ["Saving", "Spend", "Safe", "Basket", "Important"], "grammar": "If you get..., save...", "tryAtHome": "Make saving plan for week"},
        {"unit": "U52", "title": "At the Bank", "canDo": "I can talk about bank", "dialoguePlace": "Bank", "readingText": "My father opened a bank account. He shows his ID. He fills a form. He deposits money. The bank keeps it safe. He can withdraw when he needs. Banks help people save and borrow. I want to learn how banks work. John also has a bank account for his shop.", "readingWords": 56, "vocab": ["Bank", "Account", "Deposit", "Withdraw", "Borrow"], "grammar": "He deposits / He withdraws", "tryAtHome": "Explain bank to a friend"},
        {"unit": "U53", "title": "Farming for Money", "canDo": "I can talk about farming", "dialoguePlace": "Farm", "readingText": "Farmers in Burundi grow beans, maize, and bananas. They sell surplus at the market. If rains are good, harvest is good. If rains fail, life is hard. Farmers need good seeds and knowledge. Farming is not only work, it is business. John buys from farmers.", "readingWords": 56, "vocab": ["Surplus", "Harvest", "Seeds", "Knowledge", "Farmers"], "grammar": "If rains are good...", "tryAtHome": "Explain farming as business"},
        {"unit": "U54", "title": "Interview Practice", "canDo": "I can answer interview questions", "dialoguePlace": "Office", "readingText": "Interviewer: Tell me about yourself. Mary: I am Mary. I am 26, hardworking and honest. I speak Kirundi, French, and English. I am an English teacher. I like to learn. Interviewer: Why do you want this job? Mary: I want to help people and grow. I am ready to work hard. Interviewer: Good. Welcome. John also practices interviews for his business.", "readingWords": 66, "vocab": ["Interview", "Hardworking", "Honest", "Grow", "Ready"], "grammar": "Tell me about yourself", "tryAtHome": "Practice interview with friend"},
        {"unit": "U55", "title": "Online Work", "canDo": "I can talk about online work", "dialoguePlace": "Cyber Cafe", "readingText": "Today, many young adults work online. They design, translate, and teach English on the internet. They need a phone, internet, and skills. Online work can pay well, but needs discipline. You must deliver on time. You must be honest. The internet is a tool. Mary teaches English online. John sells online too.", "readingWords": 62, "vocab": ["Online", "Design", "Translate", "Deliver", "Skills"], "grammar": "They need...", "tryAtHome": "List 3 skills you can sell online"},
        {"unit": "U56", "title": "Budget for Family", "canDo": "I can make budget", "dialoguePlace": "Home", "readingText": "My family makes a budget. We list income and expenses. Food: 150,000 FBu. School: 50,000 FBu. Transport: 30,000 FBu. Savings: 20,000 FBu. If we spend more than income, we have problems. Budget helps us live in peace. Every family needs a budget. John makes a budget for his shop.", "readingWords": 60, "vocab": ["Budget", "Income", "Expenses", "Peace", "List"], "grammar": "We list / We spend", "tryAtHome": "Make budget for 100,000 FBu"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "B2.1",
      "theme": "Ideas & Opinions",
      "units": [
        {"unit": "U57", "title": "Should Phones Be Allowed?", "canDo": "I can give opinion", "dialoguePlace": "School Debate", "readingText": "Some people say phones should not be allowed in school. They distract students. Others say phones help learning. Students can search, read, and learn English. I think phones can help if used well. Teachers should teach responsible use. Banning is not the answer. Teaching is. Mary teaches responsible use.", "readingWords": 62, "vocab": ["Allowed", "Distract", "Responsible", "Banning", "Opinion"], "grammar": "I think / Some people say", "tryAtHome": "Debate phones in school"},
        {"unit": "U58", "title": "Climate Change", "canDo": "I can talk about climate", "dialoguePlace": "Hills", "readingText": "Climate change is real. Rains come late. Dry seasons are longer. Farmers suffer. Lake levels change. We must plant trees. We must stop cutting forests. We must protect water. Every person can help. If we act now, Burundi will be green.", "readingWords": 56, "vocab": ["Climate", "Suffer", "Protect", "Act", "Green"], "grammar": "If we act now...", "tryAtHome": "Plant a tree and tell why"},
        {"unit": "U59", "title": "Education for Girls", "canDo": "I can argue for education", "dialoguePlace": "Community Hall", "readingText": "Every girl deserves education. When a girl learns, she helps her family. She earns more. She makes good decisions. She teaches her children. Educating girls is not charity. It is justice. Burundi needs educated girls and boys. Let us support all children. Mary is an example.", "readingWords": 60, "vocab": ["Deserves", "Justice", "Support", "Decisions", "Educated"], "grammar": "Every girl deserves...", "tryAtHome": "Write 3 reasons girls need school"},
        {"unit": "U60", "title": "Social Media", "canDo": "I can discuss social media", "dialoguePlace": "Youth Center", "readingText": "Social media connects people. I can talk to friends in Gitega and Bujumbura. I can learn English on TikTok. But social media can also waste time. Some people compare themselves and feel sad. We must use it wisely. Follow good pages. Limit time. Life is more than likes. Mary uses it to teach.", "readingWords": 66, "vocab": ["Connects", "Waste time", "Compare", "Wisely", "Limit"], "grammar": "It can / It can also", "tryAtHome": "List good and bad of social media"},
        {"unit": "U61", "title": "Health and Food", "canDo": "I can talk about health", "dialoguePlace": "Clinic", "readingText": "Health is wealth. If we eat only chips and soda, we become sick. If we eat beans, vegetables, fruit, and fish, we become strong. We must drink clean water. We must exercise. We must sleep well. Good health needs daily choices. John eats healthy for work.", "readingWords": 58, "vocab": ["Health", "Wealth", "Exercise", "Choices", "Strong"], "grammar": "If we eat..., we become...", "tryAtHome": "Plan healthy meal"},
        {"unit": "U62", "title": "My City vs Village", "canDo": "I can compare places", "dialoguePlace": "Bus", "readingText": "City life is fast. Village life is calm. In Bujumbura, there are many jobs and schools. But there is noise and traffic. In the village, air is clean. Food is fresh. But jobs are few. I like both. City for work, village for peace. Each has beauty.", "readingWords": 58, "vocab": ["Compare", "Traffic", "Noise", "Fresh", "Peace"], "grammar": "City is faster than village", "tryAtHome": "Compare city and village"},
        {"unit": "U63", "title": "A Book That Changed Me", "canDo": "I can talk about book", "dialoguePlace": "Library", "readingText": "I read a book about a woman who never gave up. She was poor. She walked 10 km to work. She studied under a tree. She became a teacher. That book changed me. I learned that excuses do not help. Effort helps. Now I work harder. Mary shares this book with students.", "readingWords": 60, "vocab": ["Changed", "Excuses", "Effort", "Poor", "Never gave up"], "grammar": "She walked / She became", "tryAtHome": "Tell about a book you liked"},
        {"unit": "U64", "title": "Future of Burundi", "canDo": "I can talk about future", "dialoguePlace": "Youth Forum", "readingText": "In 10 years, Burundi will be different. More youth will speak English. More businesses will grow. Technology will help farmers. Schools will be better. But this future needs us. We must study, work honestly, and help each other. The future is in our hands.", "readingWords": 56, "vocab": ["Future", "Technology", "Honestly", "Hands", "Grow"], "grammar": "Burundi will be...", "tryAtHome": "Describe Burundi in 2035"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "B2.2",
      "theme": "Leadership",
      "units": [
        {"unit": "U65", "title": "What Is Leadership?", "canDo": "I can define leadership", "dialoguePlace": "School", "readingText": "Leadership is not about power. It is about service. A good leader listens. A good leader helps others grow. A good leader admits mistakes. In Burundi, we need leaders who are honest. Leaders who plant trees, not cut them. Leaders who build schools, not walls. Mary and John lead by example.", "readingWords": 62, "vocab": ["Leadership", "Service", "Listens", "Honest", "Build"], "grammar": "A good leader...", "tryAtHome": "Describe a good leader"},
        {"unit": "U66", "title": "Starting a Project", "canDo": "I can plan project", "dialoguePlace": "Community", "readingText": "Mary wants to start an English club for adults. First, she will find 10 adults. Second, she will find a room. Third, she will make a schedule. She will meet every Saturday. She will teach 20 words per week. John will help with organisation. Small projects become big. Start small, dream big.", "readingWords": 60, "vocab": ["Project", "Schedule", "Club", "Start small", "Dream big"], "grammar": "First... Second... Third...", "tryAtHome": "Plan a small project"},
        {"unit": "U67", "title": "Public Speaking", "canDo": "I can speak in public", "dialoguePlace": "Hall", "readingText": "Many adults fear public speaking. They fear mistakes. But speaking is a skill. Prepare. Practice. Breathe. Look at the audience. Speak slowly. Tell a story. Your voice matters. When you speak, Burundi listens. Do not be afraid to speak. Mary practices every week.", "readingWords": 56, "vocab": ["Fear", "Prepare", "Practice", "Audience", "Voice"], "grammar": "Prepare / Practice / Breathe", "tryAtHome": "Speak 1 minute about yourself"},
        {"unit": "U68", "title": "Solving Conflict", "canDo": "I can talk about peace", "dialoguePlace": "Neighbourhood", "readingText": "Two neighbours were fighting over land. They shouted. They stopped talking. A wise elder called them. He listened to both. He said: Land will remain, but friendship is precious. They shook hands. They shared the land. Peace needs listening, not shouting.", "readingWords": 54, "vocab": ["Conflict", "Elder", "Precious", "Listening", "Peace"], "grammar": "They were fighting", "tryAtHome": "Role play solving conflict"},
        {"unit": "U69", "title": "Entrepreneur Story", "canDo": "I can tell entrepreneur story", "dialoguePlace": "Market", "readingText": "Peter started with 20,000 FBu. He bought avocados from farmers. He sold them in Bujumbura. He saved profit. He bought more. After one year, he had a shop. After three years, he employed 5 people. He says: Start where you are. Use what you have. Do what you can. John follows his example.", "readingWords": 64, "vocab": ["Entrepreneur", "Profit", "Employed", "Start", "Shop"], "grammar": "He started / He bought", "tryAtHome": "Tell entrepreneur idea"},
        {"unit": "U70", "title": "Technology in Burundi", "canDo": "I can talk about tech", "dialoguePlace": "Cyber", "readingText": "Technology is changing Burundi. Farmers check weather on phones. Adults learn English on YouTube. Mothers send money by mobile. But many villages still have no internet. We need to connect everyone. Technology should help all, not only cities.", "readingWords": 56, "vocab": ["Technology", "Connect", "Mobile money", "Internet", "Changing"], "grammar": "Technology is changing...", "tryAtHome": "List 3 tech benefits"},
        {"unit": "U71", "title": "My Opinion Essay", "canDo": "I can write opinion", "dialoguePlace": "Class", "readingText": "In my opinion, English is important for Burundi. Firstly, it connects us to East Africa. Secondly, it helps us get jobs. Thirdly, it helps us learn from the world. Some people say French is enough. I disagree. English opens doors. Every adult should learn English.", "readingWords": 58, "vocab": ["Opinion", "Firstly", "Secondly", "Disagree", "Opens doors"], "grammar": "In my opinion...", "tryAtHome": "Write 5 sentences opinion"},
        {"unit": "U72", "title": "Preparing for Exam", "canDo": "I can plan study", "dialoguePlace": "Library", "readingText": "Exams are near. I make a plan. Morning: English. Afternoon: Maths. Evening: Revision. I study 2 hours, then rest. I do not cram. I sleep early. I eat well. Success needs plan, not panic. If I prepare, I will pass. John prepares for his business exam too.", "readingWords": 58, "vocab": ["Prepare", "Revision", "Cram", "Panic", "Success"], "grammar": "I will / If I prepare...", "tryAtHome": "Make study plan"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "C1.1",
      "theme": "Critical Thinking",
      "units": [
        {"unit": "U73", "title": "Media Literacy", "canDo": "I can analyse media", "dialoguePlace": "Youth Center", "readingText": "Not everything on the internet is true. Some news is fake. Some videos are edited. We must ask: Who wrote this? What is the source? Is there evidence? Critical thinking protects us. Do not share before you verify. Truth matters.", "readingWords": 54, "vocab": ["Media", "Fake", "Source", "Evidence", "Verify"], "grammar": "Not everything is true", "tryAtHome": "Check 1 news source"},
        {"unit": "U74", "title": "The Power of Language", "canDo": "I can discuss language", "dialoguePlace": "University", "readingText": "Language shapes thought. Kirundi carries our wisdom. French connects us to history. English connects us to the world. Learning English does not mean forgetting Kirundi. It means adding a tool. A person with three languages has three worlds.", "readingWords": 54, "vocab": ["Shapes", "Wisdom", "Carries", "Adding", "Worlds"], "grammar": "Language shapes...", "tryAtHome": "Say proverb in 2 languages"},
        {"unit": "U75", "title": "Poverty and Solutions", "canDo": "I can discuss poverty", "dialoguePlace": "Forum", "readingText": "Poverty is not only lack of money. It is lack of opportunity, education, and health. Solutions need more than aid. They need skills, jobs, and honest leadership. When youth learn, earn, and build, poverty decreases. We must not wait for help. We must create.", "readingWords": 56, "vocab": ["Poverty", "Opportunity", "Aid", "Decreases", "Create"], "grammar": "Poverty is not only...", "tryAtHome": "Propose 1 solution to poverty"},
        {"unit": "U76", "title": "History Matters", "canDo": "I can talk about history", "dialoguePlace": "Museum", "readingText": "History teaches us. Burundi has a rich history of kings, drums, and resilience. We must learn from past mistakes and past wisdom. If we forget history, we repeat mistakes. If we remember, we build better. History is not only past, it is a guide.", "readingWords": 54, "vocab": ["History", "Resilience", "Guide", "Remember", "Repeat"], "grammar": "If we forget...", "tryAtHome": "Tell 1 history fact"},
        {"unit": "U77", "title": "Women in Leadership", "canDo": "I can discuss gender", "dialoguePlace": "Parliament", "readingText": "Women lead families, businesses, and communities. Yet many still face barriers. When women lead, decisions become more balanced. Schools improve. Health improves. Gender equality is not about competition. It is about using all talents. Burundi needs women leaders. Mary is a women leader.", "readingWords": 58, "vocab": ["Barriers", "Balanced", "Equality", "Talents", "Lead"], "grammar": "When women lead...", "tryAtHome": "Name a woman leader"},
        {"unit": "U78", "title": "Ethics and Honesty", "canDo": "I can discuss ethics", "dialoguePlace": "Office", "readingText": "Corruption destroys trust. If a leader steals, schools suffer. If a teacher sells grades, students suffer. Honesty is not easy, but it is powerful. A society built on honesty grows slowly but surely. I choose honesty, even when it is hard.", "readingWords": 52, "vocab": ["Ethics", "Corruption", "Trust", "Destroys", "Honesty"], "grammar": "If a leader steals...", "tryAtHome": "Give example of honesty"},
        {"unit": "U79", "title": "Science and Innovation", "canDo": "I can talk about science", "dialoguePlace": "Lab", "readingText": "Science helps farmers grow more. It helps doctors cure diseases. It helps us understand climate. Burundi needs scientists, engineers, and innovators. Innovation does not need big labs. It needs curiosity. A young adult with a question can change the world.", "readingWords": 56, "vocab": ["Science", "Innovation", "Curiosity", "Cure", "Engineers"], "grammar": "Science helps...", "tryAtHome": "Ask a science question"},
        {"unit": "U80", "title": "Debate: Tradition vs Modern", "canDo": "I can debate", "dialoguePlace": "School Hall", "readingText": "Should we keep all traditions? Some say traditions are our identity. Others say some traditions block progress. I think we must keep good traditions like respect and drums, and change harmful ones. Tradition and progress can live together. Wisdom is choosing.", "readingWords": 56, "vocab": ["Tradition", "Identity", "Progress", "Harmful", "Wisdom"], "grammar": "Should we keep...?", "tryAtHome": "Debate tradition vs modern"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "C1.2",
      "theme": "Global Citizen",
      "units": [
        {"unit": "U81", "title": "East Africa Community", "canDo": "I can talk about EAC", "dialoguePlace": "Arusha", "readingText": "Burundi is part of the East African Community. We trade with Kenya, Tanzania, Uganda, Rwanda, DRC, Somalia, and South Sudan. English helps us trade. It helps us travel. It helps us study. Being East African means we are stronger together.", "readingWords": 54, "vocab": ["Community", "Trade", "Stronger", "Together", "Travel"], "grammar": "We trade with...", "tryAtHome": "Name EAC countries"},
        {"unit": "U82", "title": "Migration Stories", "canDo": "I can discuss migration", "dialoguePlace": "Airport", "readingText": "Many Burundians live abroad. Some left for study. Some for work. Some for safety. They send money home. They miss home. Migration is hard. It brings opportunity and pain. Home remains in the heart. Wherever we go, we carry Burundi.", "readingWords": 54, "vocab": ["Migration", "Abroad", "Opportunity", "Pain", "Carry"], "grammar": "Some left for...", "tryAtHome": "Tell migration story"},
        {"unit": "U83", "title": "Climate Justice", "canDo": "I can argue climate justice", "dialoguePlace": "Conference", "readingText": "Africa produces little pollution, but suffers most from climate change. This is unfair. Rich countries must help. Burundi must also act. We must plant, protect, and adapt. Climate justice means everyone takes responsibility. The earth belongs to all.", "readingWords": 52, "vocab": ["Justice", "Pollution", "Unfair", "Adapt", "Responsibility"], "grammar": "Africa produces little...", "tryAtHome": "Argue for climate justice"},
        {"unit": "U84", "title": "The Future of Work", "canDo": "I can talk about future work", "dialoguePlace": "Office", "readingText": "In the future, many jobs will change. AI will do routine tasks. Humans will need creativity, empathy, and problem-solving. English plus tech plus honesty will be powerful. We must learn continuously. Learning never stops.", "readingWords": 52, "vocab": ["Future", "Creativity", "Empathy", "Problem-solving", "Continuously"], "grammar": "AI will do...", "tryAtHome": "List future skills"},
        {"unit": "U85", "title": "Writing a CV", "canDo": "I can write CV", "dialoguePlace": "Career Center", "readingText": "A good CV is clear. Name, contact, education, skills, experience. Use action verbs: managed, taught, organised. Keep it one page. Tailor it to the job. Proofread. A CV is your story on paper. Make it honest and strong. Mary helps adults write CVs.", "readingWords": 54, "vocab": ["CV", "Experience", "Tailor", "Proofread", "Action verbs"], "grammar": "Use action verbs", "tryAtHome": "Draft your CV"},
        {"unit": "U86", "title": "Negotiation", "canDo": "I can negotiate", "dialoguePlace": "Market", "readingText": "Negotiation is not fighting. It is finding win-win. Listen. Understand needs. Propose options. Be respectful. Be firm on value. If you sell bananas, know your price. If you buy, know your budget. Good negotiation builds long-term relationships. John negotiates every day.", "readingWords": 58, "vocab": ["Negotiation", "Win-win", "Propose", "Firm", "Relationships"], "grammar": "Listen / Understand / Propose", "tryAtHome": "Practice negotiation"},
        {"unit": "U87", "title": "Presenting an Idea", "canDo": "I can present idea", "dialoguePlace": "Conference Hall", "readingText": "To present an idea: Start with a problem. Show your solution. Show evidence. Show benefits. End with a call to action. Speak clearly. Use visuals. Answer questions calmly. Your idea deserves to be heard. Prepare, then shine.", "readingWords": 52, "vocab": ["Present", "Solution", "Evidence", "Benefits", "Call to action"], "grammar": "Start with...", "tryAtHome": "Present an idea in 2 min"},
        {"unit": "U88", "title": "Global English", "canDo": "I can discuss Global English", "dialoguePlace": "Online", "readingText": "English is a global language. It has many accents: British, American, Kenyan, Indian, Burundian. All are valid. What matters is clarity and kindness. Do not fear accent. Fear silence. Speak. Your English is your bridge to the world.", "readingWords": 52, "vocab": ["Global", "Accents", "Valid", "Clarity", "Bridge"], "grammar": "All are valid", "tryAtHome": "Record yourself speaking"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "level": "C2.1",
      "theme": "Mastery",
      "units": [
        {"unit": "U89", "title": "Philosophy of Ubuntu", "canDo": "I can discuss Ubuntu", "dialoguePlace": "Village Circle", "readingText": "Ubuntu says: I am because we are. A person is people. My success is linked to yours. If my neighbour suffers, I suffer. Ubuntu teaches community, not selfishness. Modern life pushes individualism. Ubuntu reminds us to return to togetherness.", "readingWords": 52, "vocab": ["Ubuntu", "Linked", "Selfishness", "Togetherness", "Individualism"], "grammar": "I am because we are", "tryAtHome": "Explain Ubuntu in English"},
        {"unit": "U90", "title": "Critical Essay: Development", "canDo": "I can write critical essay", "dialoguePlace": "University", "readingText": "Development is not only buildings. It is human freedom. A country can have tall buildings and still have hungry children. True development means children learn, mothers are healthy, farmers earn fairly. We must measure development by wellbeing, not only by GDP.", "readingWords": 56, "vocab": ["Development", "Freedom", "Wellbeing", "Measure", "Fairly"], "grammar": "Development is not only...", "tryAtHome": "Write paragraph on development"},
        {"unit": "U91", "title": "Storytelling as Power", "canDo": "I can analyse storytelling", "dialoguePlace": "Library", "readingText": "Stories shape nations. Grandmothers stories taught values. Today, films and social media tell stories. Who tells the story controls the narrative. Burundians must tell their own stories. In English and Kirundi. Our stories are not poverty. Our stories are resilience, drums, hills, and hope.", "readingWords": 58, "vocab": ["Narrative", "Controls", "Resilience", "Values", "Shape"], "grammar": "Who tells... controls...", "tryAtHome": "Tell your story in 3 min"},
        {"unit": "U92", "title": "Research Skills", "canDo": "I can do research", "dialoguePlace": "Library", "readingText": "Research starts with a question. Find sources. Compare sources. Take notes. Cite sources. Do not copy. Think. A good researcher is honest. If you use someone else's words, give credit. Research is not copying, it is creating understanding.", "readingWords": 52, "vocab": ["Research", "Sources", "Cite", "Credit", "Understanding"], "grammar": "Find / Compare / Cite", "tryAtHome": "Research 1 topic"},
        {"unit": "U93", "title": "Leadership Speech", "canDo": "I can write speech", "dialoguePlace": "Hall", "readingText": "Fellow Burundians, we stand at a crossroads. Our hills are green. Our youth are bright. Our future is ours. We must choose honesty over corruption, work over excuses, unity over division. Let us build a Burundi where every child learns, every farmer earns, every voice counts. The time is now.", "readingWords": 56, "vocab": ["Fellow", "Crossroads", "Unity", "Division", "Counts"], "grammar": "We must choose...", "tryAtHome": "Write 1 min speech"},
